In the setting of spinal tuberculosis in the upper lumbar and lower thoracic spine, radiographs serve as the primary diagnostic tool, demonstrating 82% sensitivity, 57% specificity, and 73% accuracy. Characteristic radiographic findings include rarefaction of vertebral end plates, disk height loss, osseous destruction, new bone formation, and soft tissue abscess, often leading to gibbus deformity and vertebral collapse. Additionally, concomitant pulmonary tuberculosis is common, with up to 67% of patients having associated primary lung focus or a history of pulmonary tuberculosis.
